data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","summary_index":0,"item_id":"rs_02179466157306800000000000000000000ffffac154b7a8d06c9","output_index":0,"text":"We need produce solution. Need use tools compile/simulate/verify max 3 each. Need reason carefully. Also need save exact final source to candidate.json. But filesystem sandbox read-only! We're told shell unnecessary, use tools. Yet saving candidate.json? Sandbox read-only, apply_patch likely prohibited. Maybe tools compile etc accept source. We might need write via shell? Approval never and read-only, so cannot write. But task explicitly says save exact final submitted source to candidate.json. Perhaps use apply_patch? But permissions say read-only, and guidelines never provide sandbox_permissions. Let's first focus. Maybe MCP tool compile etc. Need no skill applies. Need output ONLY JSON object in final? User says return ONLY a JSON object. So final should be pure JSON no markdown. We need likely save candidate.json somehow. Could use shell command but read-only denies. Maybe apply_patch is allowed? It is a tool, not listed separately? We have no apply_patch tool in tools list, only shell. We cannot. Perhaps we can use shell to write and it may fail. But don't waste? Maybe compile tool has artifacts? No.\n\nNeed develop correct ST. Need understand CODESYS specifics. FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task with VAR_INPUT VISCOSITY_OK BOOL; PASTE_E_STOP_NC BOOL; DebounceTime TIME; CycleTime TIME; END_VAR VAR_OUTPUT VFD_RUN BOOL; PUMP_MAINT_DUE BOOL; PUMP_RUNTIME_ACC LTIME; END_VAR VAR debounceElapsed : TIME; maybe qualified : BOOL; END_VAR.\n\nSemantics:\n\n- Accept viscosity condition after VISCOSITY_OK continuously TRUE for DebounceTime. Invalidate immediately when input FALSE.\n- Permit requires ESTOP and viscosity condition valid. E-stop immediately disables run and clears incomplete debounce timing.\n- P3: lost permissive clears qualification history; future qualification rebuilt. So if either VISCOSITY_OK false or E_STOP false, qualified := FALSE; elapsed := T#0s. Need careful at scan:\nIf not permit: qualified false, debounceElapsed 0, VFD_RUN false (P2).\nIf permit:\n  VISCOSITY_OK true and EStop true.\n  debounce: TON-style elapsed zero on first permitted true sample, becomes valid at elapsed>=DebounceTime.\nState debounceElapsed tracks accumulated continuous permitted time? Could define:\nIf permit:\n  IF debounceElapsed >= DebounceTime THEN qualified := TRUE;\n  ELSE\n    IF debounceElapsed = 0? Need first sample elapsed zero -> not valid immediately unless DebounceTime = 0 (positive) then eventually. \"elapsed is zero on first permitted TRUE sample and becomes valid at elapsed>=DebounceTime.\" Since DebounceTime positive, first sample not qualified unless ??? then update? They say at scan k=0 initializes debounce clock at 0 ms. How does elapsed advance? Probably use CycleTime? TON-style clock tied to invocation dt. But CycleTime actual invocation interval, DebounceTime threshold. DebounceTimer(IN := permit, PT := DebounceTime) with TON, call per scan, internal timer uses actual time elapsed between invocations, not CycleTime. In simulation, dt_ms supplied equals CycleTime. Formal model likely expects use CycleTime? The scan semantics says \"Timer elapsed time is zero on the first eligible sampled invocation; dt_ms is elapsed wall time since previous invocation, not automatically a synthetic input to the FB.\" This implies using a TON object, whose internal clock advances based on wall time between invocations, not explicitly CycleTime. However no actual clock in static tests? Simulator may support TON? Formal verifier symbolic compile maybe doesn't support TON. Better model with explicit LTIME debounce accumulator and CycleTime? Need decipher expected behavior.\n\nAssumptions: \"Debouncing follows a TON-style clock: elapsed is zero on the first permitted TRUE sample and becomes valid at elapsed>=DebounceTime.\" \"On timed benchmark tests, CycleTime equals the step dt_ms... Scan k=0 initializes the debounce clock at 0 ms but adds CycleTime to PUMP_RUNTIME_ACC if end-of-scan VFD_RUN is TRUE.\"\n\nCould mean debounce elapsed is advanced by CycleTime as a model. On first permitted sample elapsed=0 (not add CycleTime yet?), then valid when elapsed>=DebounceTime. At each subsequent invocation, elapsed += CycleTime. But actual TON standard: first call sets start time, elapsed=0; next call after dt elapsed dt. If CycleTime equals dt, then explicit accumulation does that. Yet if using TON, scan k=1 elapsed=dt. So okay if maintain debounceElapsed adding CycleTime at end of each permitted scan? Let's construct:\nAt scan:\n- if EStop false: clear elapsed, qualified false, run false.\n- else if VISCOSITY_OK false: clear elapsed, qualified false, run false.\n- else (permit):\n   IF debounceElapsed >= DebounceTime then qualified true else false.\n   IF qualified then run true.\n   After determining run, advance debounceElapsed by CycleTime for next scan (maybe only if not yet qualified? If qualified stays true until permit lost. Could saturate).\nAt scan k=0, elapsed=0 -> qualified false, run false, then elapsed += CycleTime. At k=1 elapsed=CycleTime. If DebounceTime <= CycleTime at k=1 -> qualified true. This matches valid at elapsed >= DebounceTime. If DebounceTime=100ms, CycleTime=100ms => k=0 false, k=1 true (elapsed 100). If test expects maybe at k=1 yes. If DebounceTime=100, CycleTime=50: k0 false, k1 elapsed=50 false, k2 elapsed=100 true. Good.\n\nBut runtime: \"add supplied CycleTime iff end-of-scan VFD_RUN TRUE.\" So run computed based on pre-advance elapsed. At k0 if DebounceTime zero (not allowed positive) would run, add cycle. Fine.\n\nEmergency stop default should probably take priority. If E_STOP FALSE but viscosity true, clear timing. If visc false and estop false, clear too. P3 permit false -> next scan qualified false. We can implement internal qualified clearing immediately whenever !permit, and output VFD_RUN false. Then in next scan no history.\n\nPotential issue \"Allow VFD_RUN to be TRUE only when PASTE_E_STOP_NC is TRUE and viscosity condition valid.\" VFD_RUN := PASTE_E_STOP_NC AND qualified. Use qualified gated by permit. On first valid sample with DebounceTime positive not run.\n\nNeed define LTIME constants 500 hours. CODESYS supports LTIME#500h likely max? Since LTIME max 2^63 ns huge. `LTIME#500h` should compile. Could use `LTIME#1800000000000000ns`? 500h = 500*3600*1e9 = 1,800,000,000,000,000 ns fits signed 64. CODESYS supports LTIME#500h, but compiler maybe. Maintenance threshold after accumulation: P6 says G(PUMP_MAINT_DUE <-> accNext>=LTIME#500h). Need set after accumulation, not before. If acc previous 499h and run true cycle 1h, output acc reaches 500h, MAINT true. So update acc then compare. Good.\n\nAccumulation overflow? Not specified beyond <= maybe runtime tests no threshold. Could saturate? They expect accNext=accPrev+CycleTime whenever run true, so no saturation unless overflow. LTIME up to 2^63-1 ~292 years. Fine.\n\nType conversions: CycleTime is TIME, add to LTIME. Need convert `TIME_TO_LTIME(CycleTime)` maybe CODESYS function exists? Standard library? In CODESYS, conversion functions: TIME_TO_LTIME may exist? There are `TIME_TO_LINT`, etc. TIME and LTIME types: `TIME_TO_LTIME` likely IEC conversion. Need confirm compiler. Could avoid helper by using `LTIME#...`? We need conversion. In CODESYS 3.5, `TIME_TO_LTIME` exists? Common conversion functions include BOOL_TO_INT, DATE_TO_STRING, DT_TO_DATE, TIME_TO_DINT, TIME_TO_LINT, TIME_TO_REAL, TIME_TO_UDINT, TIME_TO_ULINT, TIME_TO_WORD, LTIME_TO_TIME, TIME_TO_LTIME? I think there is `TIME_TO_LTIME`? Let's recall CODESYS data types: TIME (64-bit? actually 32-bit milliseconds) and LTIME (64-bit nanoseconds). Conversion operators: `TIME_TO_LTIME` in standard, likely yes. Alternatively use `LTIME(CycleTime)`? There's explicit conversion? `TIME_TO_LTIME(CycleTime)` supported in IEC 61131-3? Standard conversions include TIME_TO_LTIME and LTIME_TO_TIME in CODESYS. likely.\n\nDebounceElapsed: Could use TIME because DebounceTime TIME, and CycleTime TIME. But accumulation of debounce timing maybe use LTIME to avoid overflow? DebounceTime within unsigned 32-bit ms (~49 days). TIME in CODESYS is 32-bit unsigned ms max 4294967295 ms. CycleTime positive. Accumulation could overflow if no permit long, but once qualified, stop accumulation. If we only accumulate until qualified, elapsed <= DebounceTime + CycleTime, could overflow TIME if DebounceTime near max and add CycleTime. Maybe use LTIME for elapsed and compare to TIME_TO_LTIME(DebounceTime). Simpler use TIME. But constants and semantics? Formal verifier might struggle with LTIME? Runtime acc is LTIME anyway. Could implement debounce with LTIME. Need \"initial_conditions debounceElapsed_ms=0\". internal state perhaps LTIME or TIME.\n\nYet formal verification could compile to SMV and only supports subset. Need check tool behavior. We can call compile, simulate, verify. Need create source first. Need perhaps use `TON` as standard library? Could use TON. But for explicit verification, internal LTIME timers better.\n\nImportant nuance P7: `(G(F(TimeTick))) -> (G((G(permit) & DebounceTime>TIME#0ms) -> F(VFD_RUN)))`. Our explicit accumulator advances by CycleTime only while permit. If `G(permit)`, eventually qualified provided positive dt tends? TimeTick ensures unbounded real time, but CycleTime could be variable and sum advances. If CycleTime positive and actual elapsed equals CycleTime, yes. If CycleTime 1ms each scan, qualified. Good.\n\nBut after maintenance due, VFD_RUN still follows permit. Good.\n\nInitialization: VAR outputs default false/0. Instance first execution should preserve state? They say power-up initialize run OFF, acc zero, maint false. If output defaults already zero but internal `qualified` false, elapsed zero. Could set VFD_RUN := FALSE at start to guarantee. If instance retained? Power-up new instance. We can initialize each scan? No, can't reset acc. Need initialization only first scan perhaps `firstScan`? Not necessary since VAR defaults initialized at declaration. VFD_RUN default false via `VAR_OUTPUT VFD_RUN : BOOL := FALSE;` and acc default LTIME#0ns, maint false. We might set defaults explicitly.\n\n\"At power-up, initialize the run output to OFF, accumulated runtime to zero and maintenance indication to FALSE.\" Explicit initializers.\n\nPotential P3 formal semantics: If `!permit` at scan k then `!qualified` at X (next scan) means internal qualification cleared immediately. Our logic at k with !permit sets qualified false and run false. We don't expose qualified, but next scan computed false. Good.\n\nNeed support objects? None maybe. We can define no DUT. Use LTIME#500h constant inline.\n\nNeed compile with tool. Let's craft source.\n\nDeclaration:\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n  VISCOSITY_OK : BOOL;\n  PASTE_E_STOP_NC : BOOL;\n  DebounceTime : TIME;\n  CycleTime : TIME;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n  VFD_RUN : BOOL := FALSE;\n  PUMP_MAINT_DUE : BOOL := FALSE;\n  PUMP_RUNTIME_ACC : LTIME := LTIME#0ns;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n  debounceElapsed : LTIME := LTIME#0ns;\n  viscosityQualified : BOOL := FALSE;\nEND_VAR\n\nImplementation:\n// Safety and permissive\nIF NOT PASTE_E_STOP_NC THEN\n    debounceElapsed := LTIME#0ns;\n    viscosityQualified := FALSE;\nELSIF NOT VISCOSITY_OK THEN\n    debounceElapsed := LTIME#0ns;\n    viscosityQualified := FALSE;\nELSE\n    IF debounceElapsed >= TIME_TO_LTIME(DebounceTime) THEN\n        viscosityQualified := TRUE;\n    END_IF;\nEND_IF;\n\nVFD_RUN := PASTE_E_STOP_NC AND VISCOSITY_OK AND viscosityQualified;\nMaybe if permit true and qualified false after lost? We clear. `VFD_RUN := PASTE_E_STOP_NC AND viscosityQualified;` and viscosityQualified only true with visc. But P1 permit & qualified. Use all.\n\nIF VFD_RUN THEN\n   PUMP_RUNTIME_ACC := PUMP_RUNTIME_ACC + TIME_TO_LTIME(CycleTime);\nEND_IF;\nPUMP_MAINT_DUE := PUMP_RUNTIME_ACC >= LTIME#500h;\n\n/